<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · c. 1850 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Here We Come A-wassailing</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old English song that singers took from door to door at Christmas and New Year, wishing good health.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English wassailers, who sang from door to door at Christmas and New Year</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>at least the mid 19th century, probably much older</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; dates from at least the mid 19th century)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/here-we-come-a-wassailing">Here We Come A-wassailing</a>&quot;, also known as &quot;Here We Come A-Caroling&quot;, &quot;Here We Come A-Christmasing&quot;, &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassail">Wassail</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; and by many other names, is a traditional English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> and New Year song. It was typically sung while wassailing: going from door to door singing carols, wishing good health and exchanging gifts. It is number 209 in the Roud Folk Song Index. The song dates from at least the mid 19th century but is probably much older; the &quot;a-&quot; of its title is an archaic intensifying prefix.</p><p>Reader's Digest describes the custom behind it: at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> the rich were often a little more generous than usual, and bands of beggars and orphans danced through the snowy streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, offering to sing good cheer and tell good fortune if the householder would give them a drink from his wassail bowl, a penny, a pork pie, or a few minutes beside the warmth of his hearth. The wassail bowl held a mixture of hot ale or beer, apples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and mead.</p><p>Hundreds of versions of wassailing songs have been collected, among them dozens of variants noted by Cecil Sharp from the 1900s to the 1920s, mostly in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Many are not of this type; &quot;Gower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassail">Wassail</a>&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a> Wassail&quot; are similar songs. Versions close to the one now famous were recorded from Emily <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Bromsberrow Heath, Gloucestershire, in 1952, Dorothy Davey of Hull in 1969, George Dunn of Quarry Bank, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a>, in 1971 and Frank Hinchliffe of Sheffield in 1976. The song appears to have travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> with English settlers: it has been found several times in the Appalachian region and was recorded twice there, from Edith Fitzpatrick James of Ashland, Kentucky, in 1934 and from Jean Ritchie of Viper, Kentucky, in 1949.</p><p>The song has many variants. In 1949 the Welsh folk singer Phil Tanner sang a minor-key variant called &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassail">Wassail</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;, generally known as &quot;Gower Wassail&quot;, which folk revival groups made popular. &quot;Here We Come A-Christmasing&quot; replaces the word wassail with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. Other variants, often though not always sung by Americans, begin and are titled &quot;Here We Come A-caroling&quot;; they often leave out a verse and change the second line of the chorus. Further variants are called &quot;We've Been a While-A-Wandering&quot; and &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> Wassail Song&quot;, and satirical ones were used in the 1987 television special A Claymation Christmas Celebration.</p><p>As with most carols there are several related versions of the words; a standard one is given in The New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> Book of Carols. The verses are sung in 6/8 time and the chorus changes to common time.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The singers go from house to house, greet the master and mistress, ask for a share of food, drink and a little money, and wish the household good health and joy for the season. It is a cheerful song of asking and blessing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Here we come a-wassailing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Among the leaves so green</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Here we come a-wand'rin</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So fair to be seen.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Love and joy come to you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And to you your wassail too;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And God bless you and send you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A Happy New Year</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And God send you a Happy New Year</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our wassail cup is made</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of the rosemary tree,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And so is our beer</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of the best barley:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We are not daily beggars</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That beg from door to door</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But we are neighbours' children,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Whom you have seen before.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Good master and good mistress,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>While you're sitting by the fire</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Pray think of us poor children</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who wander in the mire.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God bless the master of this house</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Likewise the mistress too!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the little children</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>That 'round the table go.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; as English Wikisource gives it. Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Here_We_Come_A-Wassailing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
